# HakiOS fragment: the Samsung Exynos 9810 (S5E9810) -- what is true of the SoC, whatever phone it is in.
# Four Cortex-A55s and four Exynos M3s; the kernel takes the four A55s, the loader's platform_starlte numbers no more (the M3s are a
# cluster of their own in MPIDR, and implement ARMv8.0 where the A55s and the iPhone's A11 implement 8.2). The interrupt controller is
# a GIC-400 (GICv2), which osfmk/arm64/gic_v3_irq.c drives when the loader says so. None of the common repository's SoC drivers is
# this SoC's: they are Qualcomm's.
CONFIG_SOC="exynos9810"
# Two cores, A55s 2 and 3 (seen on the phone, 30 October 2026): S-Boot parks core 1 before the hand-over, so it never comes
# when asked, and the firmware's CPU_ON for an M3 never returns (patch 1505's log: the boot processor stays inside it), most
# likely because six PMU fields of the big cluster that Samsung's kernel writes first were never written (m3_prep=N,
# drivers/core/hakios.exynos_m3.c; README.md, The cores). iOS boots on the two.
CONFIG_CPUS=2
# The kernel on core 2, the loader watching from core 0: a kernel that hangs has its log written into Samsung's pstore RAM
# before the phone restarts (the loader's platform_starlte.log_other_cpu), and the storage needs core 0 anyway (ufs=2).
CONFIG_BOOTCPU=2
# CONFIG_CPUFREQ is not set
# CONFIG_SOCINFO is not set
# CONFIG_THERMAL is not set
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_CPUFREQ is not set
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_SOCINFO is not set
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_THERMAL is not set
# The SoC's temperatures, the Exynos 9810's TMU (drivers/power/hakios.exynos_tmu.c): built in, started by tmu=N (1 reads,
# 2 publishes kern.hakios.temp, 3 ends the run at tmu_stop=110 C or the battery's tmu_batt_stop=60 C). Not on the command
# line by default: not yet seen on a phone. The POCO's THERMAL above is Qualcomm's TSENS, not this.
CONFIG_HAKIOS_EXYNOS_TMU=y
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_PMIC is not set
